==Trivia==
*Among the first seven [[:Category:Advent Bosses|Advent Bosses]], Queen B appears to represent the sin of lust. Real queen bees are the sole reproductive females in their hives, and Queen B's debut stage is named [[Honey Trap (Deadly)|Honey Trap]], a stratagem which involves using an attractive person to lure the victim into a false relationship for other underlying purposes. She also happens to be blue, the color traditionally associated with lust.
**Canto V of Dante's ''[[wikipedia:Inferno (Dante)|Inferno]]'', which focuses on the second circle of Hell (lust), has been called the "canto of the ''queens''" due to the abundance of rulers amongst the lustful. In the second circle, lustful souls are blown about by violent, hurricane-like winds, which might be represented by Queen B's [[:Category:Floating Enemies|Floating]] trait and the [[Brollow]]s in Honey Trap and [[Honey Drip (Deadly)|Honey Drip]].
*Queen B may be based on various blue bee species such as the [[wikipedia:Osmia lignaria|blue orchard bee]], [[wikipedia:Ceratina cyanea|blue carpenter bee]], [[wikipedia:Osmia caerulescens|blue mason bee]], [[wikipedia:Osmia ribifloris|blueberry bee]], [[wikipedia:Thyreus nitidulus|neon cuckoo bee]], ''[[wikipedia:Aglae|Aglae caerulea]]'', or any number of [[wikipedia:Zonamegilla|blue-banded bees]].
*Mitsuko is a feminine Japanese name that can be spelled in a number of different ways; most commonly 光子, meaning "shining child". In this case, it's spelled as 蜜子, which means "honey child/nectar child".
*Her Japanese name may also be a reference to the late actress [[wikipedia:Mitsuko Mori|Mitsuko Mori]] (森 光子). Aside from the punny spelling of Mitsuko mentioned above, the only difference is that Queen B is Mori ''no'' Mitsuko (森の蜜子, "Mitsuko of the Forest") while the actress is just Mori Mitsuko (family names come first in Japanese).
*The first half of her Japanese description may be a reference to the title track of [[wikipedia:Ringo Sheena|Ringo Sheena]]'s second single, "[[wikipedia:Kabukichō no Joō|Kabukichō no Joō]]". The song is about a woman who becomes the new "Queen" of [[wikipedia:Kabukichō|Kabukichō]] after being abandoned by the previous Queen, her own mother.
*Since her Japanese description mentions the entertainment industry, she might be a reference to the singer [[wikipedia:Beyoncé|Beyoncé]], whose nickname is Queen B.
*Most of her stats contain repeating numbers.
**In particular, her minimum effective range of 888 may play off of how the Japanese word for 8, 八 ''hachi'', sounds exactly like 蜂, meaning honeybee.
*Although her description says, "don't get too close or you'll be the victim of her tragic sting," she cannot hit units up close. Her Japanese description instead mentions her slowing down those who approach her.

